ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [
  "Part B "wins" are dominated by a trivial accounting identity: TTFT_demote = max(0, fetch - gap), so whenever fetch < gap the policy reports ~0 critical-path cost while evict-recompute is charged the full prefill. The 488x/3413x speedups in the representative table are NOT an emergent scheduling result — they are the definition of 'hidden by gap' applied to a deterministic recompute baseline (prefix_tokens/prefill_tps). Any policy that overlaps with idle time would score the same.",
  "Part A's 89.7% hide-rate is largely a sweep-design artifact: the gap median grid (0.2,0.5,1,2,5,10,30 s) is geometrically biased toward LONG gaps (5/7 cells are >=1s), and LogNormal(sigma=0.8) with median>=1s puts >85% mass above the fetch times shown. Uniform-in-log sweep cells != realistic gap mixture; the 89.7% is a property of the GRID, not of agent traffic.",
  "The 93.5% Pareto-win rate is similarly a cell-counting artifact across an arbitrary cross-product (7560 cells with no traffic-weighting). Without a workload prior over (BW, C, prefix, gap, p_rr), % of cells is not a decision-relevant statistic — the kill-corner could be 6.5% of cells but >50% of real production traffic, or vice versa.",
  "The fair-share contention model (fetch = bytes / (B_total/C)) is the *best case* for the loser: it assumes perfect link sharing, no queueing overhead, no D2H/H2D arbitration cost, no interference with active decode PCIe traffic. The RESULTS section even concedes #5 ('this sim isolates the prefetch link'). Real contention is worse, so the 6.5% loss region is a LOWER bound on the kill-corner."
]
CONFOUNDS: [
  "Re-reference probability p_rr is swept as an exogenous parameter, but in reality p_rr is CORRELATED with gap length (long gaps -> agent more likely to rewrite context -> lower p_rr) and with concurrency (high C often = many short-lived sessions = lower p_rr). Treating p_rr as independent inflates the win-region; the joint distribution likely concentrates mass IN the kill-corner.",
  "Evict-recompute baseline uses prefill_tps in {30k,60k,120k tok/s} as a constant, but real prefill throughput DEGRADES under the same concurrency C that stresses PCIe. If both baselines degrade together under load, the 'speedup' shrinks — the comparison is not apples-to-apples at fixed C.",
  "The 'agent inter-turn gap' is conflated with 'idle GPU time on this session's KV slot'. Even if a session is idle 2s wall-clock, the GPU/PCIe may be busy serving OTHER sessions during that window — the available prefetch BW in the gap is NOT B_total, it is whatever the scheduler leaves unused. The model assumes the gap is free PCIe time, which is the assumption under test, not a given.",
  "Mechanism attribution: '70% of losses are PCIe-BW-bound' is computed from the same analytic formula that defines the loss (C/p_rr*bytes > B_total*gap). This is tautological, not an independent causal test."
]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [
  "LogNormal(sigma=0.8) gap distribution — no citation to a measured agent trace; explicitly flagged as placeholder by orchestrator.",
  "Independence of (C, gap, prefix, p_rr) in the sweep — no joint distribution from a real workload.",
  "Fair-share PCIe model with no queueing/arbitration overhead; no interference with concurrent decode D2H/H2D.",
  "Re-reference == 'demoted prefix bytes are byte-identical on resume' — ignores prefix-cache invalidation from agent-side context edits/tool-result insertions between turns.",
  "Wasted-prefetch cost is modeled as link-time only, not as memory-bandwidth or scheduler-queue pressure on the GPU side.",
  "B_eff metric is reported in the claim but the L0 RESULTS body shows only TTFT-on-resume numbers; the (B_eff, TTFT) Pareto is named but the B_eff axis is not quantified in the cited table."
]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [
  "No error bars on the 89.7% / 93.5% / 6.5% headline rates. MC=2000 per cell gives ~1% SE per cell, but the AGGREGATE rates have no CI and no sensitivity to grid choice.",
  "No reporting of the loss-region MAGNITUDE distribution beyond one worst-case (13-50x worse). What is the median loss in the 6.5%? A 1.1x loss and a 50x loss are very different policy risks.",
  "No workload-weighted summary — every cell counts equally regardless of realism.",
  "No seed-variance / reproducibility report across MC seeds for the headline aggregates.",
  "No comparison to a trivial third baseline (e.g. async prefetch triggered REACTIVELY on resume request without gap-timing) to isolate the contribution of 'gap-timing' vs 'just doing prefetch at all'. The novelty claim is gap-timing, but the experiment compares demote+prefetch (any timing) vs evict-recompute — it cannot attribute the win to the timing mechanism."
]
RATIONALE: The L0 honestly characterizes a win-region and a kill-corner, and the researcher's refusal to claim universal Pareto dominance is correct and load-bearing. However, the headline 93.5% win and 89.7% hide-rate are grid-counting artifacts under independent sweeps with no workload prior, and the speedups follow tautologically from the max(0, fetch-gap) accounting once fetch<gap. The pre-registered crux (C/p_rr*bytes vs B_total*gap) is verified by the same formula that defines it — not an independent test. Critically, the experiment compares demote+prefetch vs evict-recompute but the NOVELTY claim is specifically 'AGENT-INTER-TURN-GAP-TIMED' prefetch (vs reactive/speculative prefetch from vLLM/LMCache/InfiniGen) — and there is NO baseline isolating the gap-timing contribution. Without that contrast, the L0 cannot attribute its wins to the claimed novel mechanism rather than to 'prefetch exists and DMA is fast'. YELLOW: the modeling is honest and the scoping discipline is right, but the evidence does not yet support the mechanism-attribution the claim requires; L1 must add (a) measured gap distribution, (b) reactive-prefetch baseline, (c) joint workload prior, (d) real concurrent decode+prefetch contention.
